Unreleased
---
Feature
- The library opens. It is Fluent's answer to what a control is, drawn in
  Widgets: the corner scale, the type ramp, the elevation scale and the
  duration scale that the controls in here are drawn from, and the controls
  themselves as they land. It is one of three choices an application can make
  - ribbon, Fluent, Material - not a skin for any of them.
- FluentSwitch, the toggle switch: a pill with a knob in it and a sentence
  beside it. Qt has a check box and no switch, and they are not the same
  answer - a switch says what it stands for is in force now, a check box says
  a choice was made and will be collected later. The track fills with the
  accent when it is on and the knob travels on a Fluent duration, which is a
  motion tier: reduce-motion and the theme's scale reach it without the
  control asking about either. A click and the keyboard both toggle, the
  sentence is its accessible name until the application gives it one, and the
  wash and the focus ring stay on the track.
- FluentInfoBar, a message that stays until it is dealt with. A QMessageBox is
  a window the user has to dismiss before doing anything else and a status tip
  leaves before it has been read; what sits between them - something happened,
  it matters, it does not stop you - is what this is. It carries a severity,
  and the severity is what colours it: a theme has a role for a highlight and
  none for "warning", so a bar carries those colours itself. Closing is not
  drawing it differently, it is going away.
- FluentExpander, a header that keeps a body and opens to show it. Qt's answer
  to this is a button next to a widget the application hides and shows itself,
  which is why the two disagree about where the clickable part ends and why
  the movement is instant. Here the header is the control - it is what the
  pointer reaches and what the keyboard stops at - and the body travels out
  from under it on a Fluent duration, or is simply out, under reduce-motion.
- FluentRating, a row of stars filled up to the one the user chose. Qt has no
  rating control, and the two things a rating has to answer are not answered
  by a row of buttons: how much is it now, and what would it be if I clicked
  here. The second is the hover, and it is not decoration - a rating that does
  not show what a click would do is a control the user has to click to find
  out about. The keyboard walks it, because a rating is a value in a range.
- FluentProgressRing, the arc that says how far along something is, or only
  that something is happening. QProgressBar's indeterminate mode is the
  style's to draw, so a Fluent application gets a block sweeping a track. The
  turning here is the one motion in these controls that is not a transition -
  nothing moves from one state to another, it is a loop - so it reads the
  reduce-motion switch itself and stops, because a ring that turns forever is
  what that switch asks not to be shown.
- FluentCard, the surface that sits above the page, and the lift is what says
  how far above. A card is the one place in Fluent where elevation is the
  message rather than decoration, so the shadow is the shadow of the level
  the card was given - painted by the base, cached, and given room: the card
  keeps a margin the size of its own shadow, because contents that sat on the
  shadow of the surface they are on would be sitting on nothing. It reports a
  grouping, so what is inside is announced as a section.
- FluentBadge, the info badge: a dot that says there is something, or a
  number that says how much. Qt has no badge, which is why a count ends up
  painted into a label, a button or a pixmap made at run time - three answers
  that disagree about size, colour and what happens past 99. This one is one
  answer, and it is the accent, because a badge is the application pointing at
  something.
- FluentSlider, the slider: a rail the accent fills up to the thumb, a thumb
  that rides it, and the marks underneath that say where the rail is divided -
  the one thing Fluent's slider says that Material's does not. A drag answers
  at once, because a thumb that lags the pointer is a broken slider; a step
  from the keyboard travels, because there the movement is the feedback. It
  carries a value, so it brings its own accessible face: a role and a state
  cannot say a number, and an assistive tool wants to set it back.
- FluentCommandBar, where the commands of a Fluent application live. An
  application that has chosen Fluent has no ribbon, and this is what it has
  instead: one row of commands, the ones that matter in the order they matter,
  and the ones that do not fit kept behind a button rather than dropped. A
  command here is a QAction - connect to it, disable it, check it - and what
  the bar adds is the answer to what a click does, one of four: a command that
  runs, a command that stays in force, a menu, or both in one, where the
  chevron opens the menu and the rest of it runs the command. Room is the
  bar's to answer: what does not fit goes into the overflow and comes back
  when there is room again, and the row's least is the overflow itself. The
  keyboard walks the row, the wash and the focus ring stay on the one command
  the pointer or the keyboard is on, and what is announced is a toolbar.
- FluentBreadcrumbBar, the path to where the user is, and a way back along it.
  Qt has no breadcrumb, and the nearest thing - a row of flat buttons with ">"
  labels between them - gets three things wrong: the last step is not a
  destination, the separator is part of the text an assistive tool reads, and
  the whole row is announced as a row of buttons rather than as a path. Here
  the last step is where the user already is and answers nothing; the steps
  before it are how to go back; the separator is drawn, not typed; and what is
  announced is the path in one sentence. The wash and the focus ring stay on
  the one step the pointer or the keyboard is on, not on the whole row.
- fluenttokens, the four scales Fluent arrives with, each spelled the way
  Fluent spells it and answered in the shared vocabulary: corners as radii,
  the ramp as sizes with a heavier cut, elevation as the shadow a lift casts,
  and durations mapped onto the motion tiers - which is what makes a control
  that asks here honour the reduce-motion switch and the theme's motion scale
  without asking about either.
